{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"08","section":"02","chapter_title":"Course Completion and Transfer","title":"Professional Story Across Artifacts","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting professional story across artifacts to presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_course_completion_and_transfer","cat100_supplement_professional_story_across_artifacts","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":2,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/08/01"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_8_2_professional_story_across_artifacts_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_8_2_professional_story_across_artifacts_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_8_2_professional_story_across_artifacts_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_8_2_professional_story_across_artifacts_calibration"]},"content":"# Professional Story Across Artifacts\n\n![Professional Story Across Artifacts textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/08-02-professional-story-across-artifacts.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nBy the end of CAT 100 you have a pile of files: a spreadsheet, a website, a resume, maybe an AI critique log. A pile is not a portfolio. A hiring manager who clicks through three disconnected files learns that you can use three tools, but not that you can solve a problem they care about. The difference between \"I made things\" and \"I delivered value\" is a story, and the storyboard is where you build it.\n\nA presentation storyboard is the plan, slide by slide, before you ever open PowerPoint. It forces you to decide the order in which a specific audience meets your evidence, and which artifact answers which question. The constraint that drives every choice is the audience: a marketing recruiter and a data-analytics recruiter will want the same three artifacts arranged in completely different orders. Learning to storyboard around an audience is the skill that turns scattered coursework into a single, persuasive claim.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nDevon has three strong artifacts and a showcase deck that simply names them: slide one is \"My Excel project,\" slide two is \"My website,\" slide three is \"My resume.\" During the dry run, a mock hiring manager asks, \"So what did you actually do for me?\" Devon has no answer, because the deck is a table of contents, not an argument.\n\nThe artifacts did not get worse; the framing did. Each slide describes a file instead of advancing a claim the manager cares about. The storyboard is the fix: it starts from the manager's opening question and arranges the same three artifacts so each one supplies a piece of the answer.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA storyboard is an ordered sequence of slides, planned before design, where each slide carries one claim and the slide-level evidence that proves it. Two ideas make it work.\n\nThe first is the audience constraint. You cannot order slides until you know who is watching and what they are trying to decide. A marketing recruiter is asking \"can this person turn data into a decision and communicate it?\" A data-analytics recruiter is asking \"can this person handle real data correctly?\" The same Excel model leads the marketing deck as a business outcome (\"I cut paid-channel spend 18%\") and leads the analytics deck as a method (\"I used `SUMIFS` to segment 512 records\"). Same artifact, different headline, because the audience changed.\n\nThe second is slide-level evidence: every claim slide must sit next to something inspectable, not an adjective. \"I am detail-oriented\" is an adjective. \"Pivot table reducing 512 rows to a Region by Month grid\" is evidence. The storyboard rule is one claim, one piece of evidence, one slide. This connects to the data-ink principle: a slide that is mostly decoration buries the one number that matters, so the storyboard plans the evidence first and the styling last.\n\nA storyboard also makes ordering deliberate. Summary artifacts like a resume belong last, because they recap proof the audience has already seen. Leading with the resume asks the audience to trust a list; leading with the artifacts lets them verify, then accept the summary.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A slide is titled My Excel project with the body Spreadsheet with formulas. Why does it fail the storyboard test?\" explanation=\"A storyboard slide must carry a claim the audience cares about, backed by inspectable evidence. Naming a file is a table-of-contents entry, not an argument; the fix is a claim like I cut paid-channel spend 18% next to the SUMIFS pivot.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It names a file instead of stating a claim the audience cares about.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It uses the wrong font for a marketing audience.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It should come last because the resume always leads.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example: Turning a File List into a Storyboard\n\nA student has three capstone artifacts: an Excel budget model, a GitHub Pages portfolio site, and a one-page resume. The audience for the showcase is a marketing-internship hiring manager.\n\n**Before (a file dump).** Three slides, each just naming a file:\n\n| Slide | Title | Body |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | My Excel project | \"Spreadsheet with formulas\" |\n| 2 | My website | \"HTML site on GitHub Pages\" |\n| 3 | My resume | \"Updated resume\" |\n\nThe hiring manager asks, \"So what did you actually do for me?\" The deck cannot answer, because it lists files instead of telling a story.\n\n**After (an audience-led storyboard).** Same three artifacts, reordered around one question:\n\n| Slide | Title | Slide-level evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | \"Can this candidate turn data into a decision?\" | The opening question the manager cares about |\n| 2 | \"I cut paid-channel spend 18%\" | Pivot table with `=SUMIFS(Spend,Channel,\"Paid\")` = 12,400 highlighted |\n| 3 | \"And I can ship it publicly\" | Live GitHub Pages link plus the `index.html` heading outline |\n| 4 | \"Here is the one-page summary\" | Resume line: \"Reduced paid-channel spend 18% with a pivot-table model\" |\n\nThe resume slide moves last because it summarizes proof the manager already saw. Each claim now sits next to a visible artifact, not an adjective. That ordering is the storytelling decision a reviewer can inspect.\n\nBuild the storyboard as a procedure that starts from the audience, not the file list. Predict each step before revealing it:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Storyboard three artifacts for one audience\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"You have an Excel model, a website, and a resume for a marketing recruiter. Where does the storyboard start?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"With slide one naming My Excel project\",\"With the audience’s opening question the recruiter wants answered\",\"With the resume so they trust your list first\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Start from the audience’s question: Can this candidate turn data into a decision? Every later slide answers that one question.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Assign each artifact one claim that advances the answer. The Excel model becomes I cut paid-channel spend 18%, not I know pivot tables.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Pin inspectable evidence to each claim: the SUMIFS pivot result, the live site link, the resume bullet that recaps the number.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Order proof before summary. Place the resume last because it restates results the audience already verified, then add styling sparingly.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nNow order the storyboard moves yourself:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Write the audience’s opening question at the top of the page\",\"Assign each artifact one claim that advances the answer\",\"Pin one inspectable piece of evidence to each claim\",\"Order the slides so proof precedes the summary resume\",\"Add styling last so one number per slide stays the focus\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\nThese four concepts anchor the section; trace how they connect:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_course_completion_and_transfer\",\"cat100_supplement_professional_story_across_artifacts\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nStoryboard from the audience's opening question backward to your artifacts, not from your file list forward. For each slide, apply three tests:\n\n1. **Claim test.** Does the slide title state something the audience cares about, or does it just name a file? \"I cut spend 18%\" passes; \"My Excel project\" fails.\n2. **Evidence test.** Is there one inspectable artifact on the slide that proves the claim, with the specific formula, number, or link visible?\n3. **Order test.** Does each slide build on the last toward the audience's decision, with summaries (the resume) placed after the proof they summarize?\n\nIf a slide fails any test, it is decoration, not argument, and it should be cut or rewritten.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is thinking a storyboard is about visual themes and fonts. Those come last. A storyboard is about the order of claims and the evidence behind them; you can build a strong one on index cards before opening any software.\n\nThe second misreading is believing one deck fits every audience. Reusing your marketing storyboard verbatim for an analytics interview tells the analytics recruiter you did not think about them. The artifacts stay; the headlines and order must be re-derived from the new audience's question.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nStart by writing the audience's opening question at the top of a blank page. For Devon's marketing recruiter it is \"Can this person turn data into a decision?\" Everything downstream answers that one question.\n\nNext, list your artifacts and assign each one a claim that advances the answer. The Excel model becomes \"I cut paid-channel spend 18%,\" not \"I know pivot tables.\" The website becomes \"I can ship work publicly,\" shown by a live link. Pin the exact evidence to each claim: the `SUMIFS` result, the rendered page, the resume bullet that recaps the number.\n\nThen order the slides so proof precedes summary. Place the resume last because it restates results the audience has already verified. Only after the sequence is locked do you add styling, and you add it sparingly so the one number per slide stays the focal point. The walkthrough output is a slide table like the worked example, where a reviewer can read the argument from titles alone.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, sketch the same three artifacts arranged for a different audience than the one in the example. A strong reader does not memorize Devon's marketing deck; they can re-order the same files into an analytics deck where slide two leads with method instead of outcome.\n\nA strong reader of professional story across artifacts can answer four questions:\n\n1. What is the audience's opening question, and does slide one name it?\n2. What single claim does each artifact carry, and what evidence proves it on the slide?\n3. Why are the slides in this order, and which slide summarizes the others?\n4. How would the headlines change if the audience changed from one role to another?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe storyboard is strong when a reviewer can read only the slide titles and reconstruct the argument, and when each title is backed by visible evidence rather than an adjective. The standard is a slide table with a stated audience, one claim per slide, the exact evidence pinned to each claim, and a deliberate order that ends on the summary.\n\nA useful closing note would read: \"Audience is a marketing-internship recruiter asking whether I turn data into decisions. I led with the 18% spend cut (proved by the `SUMIFS` pivot), followed with the public site as proof I can ship, and placed the resume last because it recaps numbers already shown. I rejected the resume-first slide order suggested by Copilot in PowerPoint because it would ask the recruiter to trust a list before seeing proof.\"\n\nGround the AI move in whatever current tool you actually used: as of January 2026, Copilot in PowerPoint generates an editable outline you can refine before it builds the slides, and Gamma drafts a roughly ten-slide deck with its own narrative arc from a single prompt; either will happily propose a default ordering you must judge against the audience question. Tool names and features change fast (Copilot added Agent Mode and Claude-powered agents in January 2026); the disclosure habit does not.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nKeep the three artifacts but switch the audience to a transfer-admissions committee deciding whether you are ready for upper-division coursework. The committee's question is not \"can you do the job\" but \"can you learn and document rigorously.\" The Excel slide now leads with the method and the correctness check, the website becomes evidence of self-directed publishing, and the resume becomes secondary. Re-deriving the order from the new question, rather than reshipping the marketing deck, is the test of whether you understand storyboarding as audience reasoning instead of a fixed template.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/08-02-professional-story-across-artifacts-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"08.02\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence for CAT 100 08.02. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_professional_story_across_artifacts\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence records audience, evidence source, AI/software support, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_2_professional_story_across_artifacts_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_professional_story_across_artifacts","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_professional_story_across_artifacts","cat100_supplement_course_completion_and_transfer"],"stem":"Your storyboard has three slides: 'My Excel file', 'My website', and 'My resume', each just naming the artifact. A hiring manager asks, 'What problem did you solve?' What is the storyboard missing?","options":["A title slide and a thank-you slide.","A throughline: open with the problem, then use each artifact as evidence that answers it, instead of listing files.","More slides, one per file in the project folder.","A consistent font across all three slides."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A storyboard is an argument, not a folder listing. The fix is an opening question that the three artifacts then answer in order.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_2_one_slide_per_artifact","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_2_professional_story_across_artifacts_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"A slide reads only: 'I am strong at data analysis.' Which revision adds slide-level evidence?","options":["Bold the words and add a stock photo of a chart.","Show the actual pivot table with the formula =SUMIFS(Sales,Region,\"West\") and the result 48,200 highlighted.","Change the bullet to 'I am very strong at data analysis.'","Add a second bullet that says 'data analysis is important.'"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A claim slide needs one visible artifact. Showing the real pivot output and formula turns an adjective into evidence.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_2_bullet_text_no_evidence","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_2_professional_story_across_artifacts_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"You will present the same three artifacts to (a) a marketing hiring manager and (b) a CS peer panel. How should the storyboard change between the two?","options":["It should not change; one deck fits every audience.","For the hiring manager, lead with the outcome and fit; for the peer panel, lead with the technique and how it works. Same artifacts, reordered and reframed.","Make a longer deck for the peer panel and a shorter one for the hiring manager, with no other change.","Use brighter colors for the hiring manager."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Audience is the constraint. The artifacts stay; their order and framing shift to match what each room cares about.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_2_same_deck_every_audience","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_2_professional_story_across_artifacts_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"Which storyboard note best documents why a slide order changed?","options":["'Moved slides around to look better.'","'Moved the resume slide to the end because the peer panel cares about the technique first; the website demo now sits at slide 2 as the proof.'","'Slides reordered.'","'The deck is finished.'"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A verifiable note names which slide moved, where, and the audience reason, so a reviewer can judge the storytelling decision.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_2_same_deck_every_audience","difficulty":"medium"}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"ffba4b8a6b9f71c3a3ad47d883a25d35e61284d4c46f19696e418874289a1533","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}